<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idris I of Morocco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/791">791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Idris ibn Abdallah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idris I was a descendant of the Prophet through Hasan who fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> after a failed Alid revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in 786. Welcomed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> at Walila, he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a> in 789, conquered much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and founded a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> river. He was poisoned, reportedly by an Abbasid agent, in 791.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Idris ibn Abd Allah, also called Idris the Elder, was a Hasanid, a great-grandchild of Hasan, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> and Ali and grandson of Muhammad. He was born and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. His paternal half-brothers Muhammad al-Nafs al-Zakiyya and Ibrahim had been killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> during an abortive rebellion, and his brother Yahya, who rose in revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, was forced to surrender and was afterwards persecuted and repeatedly imprisoned by Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>.</p><p>Idris himself took part, with Yahya, in the Alid uprising of 786 under al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> al-Abid. After the revolt's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fakhkh">Battle of Fakhkh</a> he escaped and hid, then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where Wadih, the local head of the caliphal postal system, helped him reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 789 Idris arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> and went on to Walīlī, the site of the Roman Volubilis. Recent excavations by the Moroccan Institute of Archaeology and University College London have found his headquarters just outside the walls of the Roman town, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a>, under Ishaq ibn Muhammad, then occupied. He married Kenza of the Awraba and fathered a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a>. This is regarded as the consolidation and birth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>, the fourth Muslim state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekor">Nekor</a>, Barghawata and Midrar. Idris conquered large parts of northern Morocco, founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> from the Sufrite Ifranid Abu Qurra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>These successes prompted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> to send emissaries to kill him. Idris died in 791 at Walīlī, allegedly poisoned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s agents. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a> was born a few months later and brought up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> under the regency of Rashid, his father's freedman and adviser; he left Walīlī for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 808 and consolidated the Idrisid state. Idris I was buried on a hill not far from Walīlī, and the site grew into the village of Moulay Idriss Zerhoun, where a zawiya centred on his mausoleum developed over the centuries and remains an important religious site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. He is regarded as the founding father of Morocco.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Idris I of Morocco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Into this country came <b>Idris ibn Abdallah</b>, a great-grandson of Hasan ibn Ali and so a descendant of Ali ibn Abi Talib and Fatima, the daughter of the prophet Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23,46&amp;year=781" aria-label="Arabian Peninsula on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Idris I of Morocco"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3909 5530h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3909 5530h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23,46&amp;year=781">What was here in 781 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
